            <ArticleTitle>[Sphenoid sinusitis with intracranial extension produced by an emergent fungus].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>158-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.otorri.2010.01.010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This is a case of fungal sphenoid sinusitis in a diabetic patient with non-specific symptoms and bone erosion radiological findings in the superior and posterior sphenoid walls. Surgical treatment was performed by transnasal endoscopic approach and voriconazole orally thereafter. The histopathological study found fungus hyphal without mucosa invasion and the molecular study determined DNA to be Phialemonium curvatum, an unusual pathogen.</AbstractText>
